To create a Discord.js v14 bot with a ban command using only slash commands, follow these steps:

1. Install required modules: Make sure you have Node.js installed and run the following command in your terminal or command prompt to install the necessary modules:
   ```sh
   npm install discord.js @discordjs/rest @discordjs/builders
   ```
2. Create a configuration file: Create a file named `config.json` and add the following JSON data to store your bot's token and guild ID (replace with your actual values):
   ```json
   {
       "token": "YOUR_DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN",
       "guildId": "YOUR_GUILD_ID" // Replace with the ID of the server where you want the command to be enabled
   }
   ```
3. Create the Discord bot: In your project directory, create a file called `index.js`. This will be the main file of your bot. Start by importing the necessary libraries and setting up the client:
   ```javascript
   const { Client, GatewayIntentBits, PermissionsBitField, SlashCommandBuilder } = require("discord.js");
   const { REST } = require("@discordjs/rest");
   const { token, guildId } = require("./config.json");
   ```
4. Define the ban command: Create a function to define your ban command as a Discord.js `SlashCommandBuilder` object. This object will be used to register the command with the bot:
   ```javascript
   function banCommand() {
       return new SlashCommandBuilder()
           .setName("ban")
           .setDescription("Bans a user from the server.")
           .addUserOption((option) =>
               option
                   .setName("user")
                   .setDescription("The user to be banned.")
                   .setRequired(true)
           )
           .addStringOption((option) =>
               option
                   .setName("reason")
                   .setDescription("The reason for the ban.")
                   .setRequired(false)
           )
           .setDefaultMemberPermissions(PermissionsBitField.Flags.BanMembers);
   }
   ```
   By using `setDefaultMemberPermissions`, you can set the minimum permissions required to execute the command. In this case, the `BanMembers` permission is needed.
5. Register the command: Create a function to register the command with Discord's API. Replace `APPLICATION_ID` with your bot's application ID from the Discord Developer Portal:
   ```javascript
   async function registerCommands() {
       const rest = new REST({ version: '14' }).setToken(token);
       const commands = [banCommand()];

       try {
           console.log("Started refreshing application (/) commands.");

           await rest.put(
               `https://discord.com/api/v9/applications/${APPLICATION_ID}/guilds/${guildId}/commands`,
               { body: commands }
           );

           console.log("Successfully registered application (/) commands.");
       } catch (error) {
           console.error(error);
       }
   }
   ```
6. Launch the bot: Finally, launch the bot using the client and the functions we've defined:
   ```javascript
   const client = new Client({
       intents: [GatewayIntentBits.Guilds, GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages],
   });

   client.on("ready", () => {
       console.log(`Bot is online and ready!`);
       registerCommands();
   });

   client.on("interactionCreate", async (interaction) => {
       if (!interaction.isChatInputCommand()) return;

       if (interaction.commandName === "ban") {
           const user = interaction.options.getUser("user");
           const member = await interaction.guild.members.fetch(user.id);
           const reason = interaction.options.getString("reason", false);

           if (!member.bannable) {
               return interaction.reply({
                   content: `I cannot ban ${user} as they are not a member of this server or have higher permissions than me.`,
                   ephemeral: true,
               });
           }

           member.ban({ days: 1, reason: reason || "No reason given" });

           await interaction.reply({
               content: `✅ ${user} has been banned from the server. Reason: \`${reason}\``,
           });
       }
   });

   client.login(token);
   ```
7. Run the bot: In your terminal or command prompt, navigate to the project folder and run the following command to start the bot:
   ```sh
   node index.js
   ```

This code creates a Discord bot that can handle the ban command using slash commands only. The command requires the user option (the user to ban) and the string option (the reason for the ban). If the user is not a member of the server or has higher permissions than the bot, the bot informs the user and does not proceed with banning the user.

